      "content": "okay, the second version is definitely safer for the lawyers, but man... i just read it to my boss and they hated it. it sounds incredibly cold. like, \"we acknowledge the issue\" is basically saying \"we don't care that your vacation was ruined.\" it's almost borderline dismissive. \n\nthe neutrality score jumped up to 0.94, which is exactly what the metric wanted, but we're losing the \"human\" part of the assignment completely. \n\nlook, we need a middle ground. i need you to look at the text again. i know the score is high, but it's too high in the wrong way. can you find a way to re-insert a sense of empathy—just enough so people don't think we're heartless—while still keeping that score above 0.8? try to find words that convey we care without triggering the \"high emotion/liability\" flags. if it's too clinical, it's a fail for the PR team.",
